    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 162127\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n225 PM MST Fri Oct 16 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Wind advisories continue across portions of the region through \nSaturday morning with gusts occasionally over 35 mph resulting in \nareas of blowing dust and difficult travel for high profile \nvehicles. \n\n- A slight chance of light rain will arrive into parts of south-\ncentral Arizona over the weekend with the better chances focused \nover higher terrain locations. \n  \n- A Pacific low pressure system moving into the Southwest during the \nmiddle of next week will lead to much better rainfall chances with \nthe possibility of a few embedded thunderstorms, as well as cooler \ntemperatures \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Sunday/... \nAs full latitudinal troughing deepens over the eastern Conus, a \nmature high amplitude Rex block remains established over the western \nhalf of the country. This pattern continues to promote strong sfc \nhigh pressure over the central Rockies/high plains, and combined \nwith inverted low pressure emanating from the Gulf of California, an \nintense pressure gradient has developed across the CWA. An H9-H8 30-\n50kt easterly LLJ has weakened early this afternoon as the thermal \nenhancement has deteriorated, but lingering gusty winds and lofted \ndust will continue to impacts many areas through the overnight. \nWhile lower elevations will experience partial decoupling and much \nweaker winds after sunset, ridge tops and the typical wind prone \nareas will likely continue to record gusty winds pushing advisory \ncriteria through the overnight. The combined effect of a \nsignificantly loosened pressure gradient and thicker clouds \ncompromising mechanical mixing will preclude advisory criteria \nbeyond Saturday morning. \n\nThe aforementioned Rex block will slowly open this weekend with the \nnegative height anomaly propagating from southern California into \nnorthern Arizona. This places south-central Arizona within a very \nfavorable left front jet quadrant with a weak cold core skirting the \nforecast area indicative of periods of robust ascent. The underlying \nsouthwest flow will also concurrently import thick moisture through \nthe H7-H5 layer such that total column PWATs reach 1\" by Saturday \nafternoon. While these parameters would generally support widespread \nlight rain, a very dry sub-cloud layer resulting from the persistent \ndownsloping easterly winds will battle the ability for precipitation \nreaching the ground. Nevertheless, given the ascent structure and \nmagnitude of midlevel moisture, some light showers should reach the \nsurface Saturday afternoon through Sunday morning with the best \nchances over higher terrain areas east of Phoenix where sfc to cloud \nbase depths are minimized. Otherwise, partial clearing will slowly \nadvance through the forecast area Sunday with moisture and ascent \nlifting into northern Arizona. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Monday through Thursday/...\nThe ridge comprising the northern portion of the Rex block will push \neastward early next week as the upper low weakens into an open wave \nand becomes absorbed into the mean southwest flow. Lingering light \nshowers may continue to affect orographically favored areas of the \neastern CWA Monday aided by the next piece of subtropical jet energy \nrounding towards the Southwest from the eastern Pacific. If activity \ndoes develop, total accumulations are expected to be fairly minimal. \nBy Tuesday, deep troughing and strong negative height anomalies will \namplify and intensify along the west coast, however confidence is \nexact the timing and depth of this feature remains low to moderate. \nAs a result, notable forecast temperature spread exists during the \nmiddle of next week, in addition to uncertainty in rainfall timing \nand amounts. At this time, the preponderance of model evidence \npoints towards Wednesday and Thursday as the most likely to \nexperience showers and embedded thunderstorms ahead of the trough \npassage and the introduction of much cooler, below normal autumnal \ntemperatures. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1805Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nStrong gusty E'rly winds and slantwise visibility reductions due to \nlofted dust will persist through the rest of the day. E'rly winds \nwill continue to gust consistently up to 20-30 kts, with occasional \nstronger gusts up to 35 kts, through this afternoon. Strong \ngustiness should subside tonight at most terminals, but elevated \nsurface speeds in the teens will likely prevail through the \novernight hours, with an occasional gust into the 20s. Gusts to 30 \nkts will still be possible at KSDL after midnight. All terminals \nwill see a resurgence of gusts up to 23-30 kts Saturday morning, but \nthen winds will gradually subside through Saturday afternoon. \nSlantwise visibility impacts are likely through at least Saturday \nmorning. Thick mid and high cloud decks will spread into the area \nthrough the day Saturday. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nBreezy conditions through this afternoon will be the main aviation \nconcern. North-northeast flow will be common throughout the period, \nwith periodic gusts upwards of 25 kts (occasionally higher) at KBLH \nand slightly weaker at KIPL. Mostly clear skies will give way to \nincreasing mid to high clouds through the afternoon at KIPL and by \nthis evening at KBLH. An isolated shower cannot be ruled out \novernight, but chances are too low for inclusion in the TAF.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nStrong east winds will continue through Saturday across southern \nArizona with the most pronounced gusts over mountain tops of eastern \ndistricts during the overnight and early morning hours. Gusts 40-45 \nmph may be common over these locations with a portion of these winds \nbuilding into lower elevations by late morning. Speeds will slowly \ndecrease through the day with even weaker winds Sunday through the \nmiddle of next week. Light showers are possible this weekend, and \nparticularly over eastern districts, but the chance for wetting \nrain is low (~10%). The strong east winds will result in minimum \nhumidity levels 20-30% Saturday, but increasing closer to a 30-50% \nrange thereafter. Overnight recovery should be good to excellent. A \nPacific weather disturbance will bring increased chances of wetting \nrainfall, breezy winds, and cooler temperatures during the latter \nhalf of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Wind Advisory until 11 AM MST Saturday for AZZ547-552-553-555- \n     557-558-560>562.\n\n     Wind Advisory until 3 PM MST this afternoon for AZZ531>534-536-\n     538-539-554-559.\n\n     Wind Advisory from 8 AM this morning to 3 PM MST this afternoon \n     for AZZ537-540>546-548>551.\n\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...18\nLONG TERM...18/Ryan \nAVIATION...Benedict\nFIRE WEATHER...18/Ryan\n",
